  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_9a9892898b414938b24e09bea97be954",
      "statement": "The packed pose array is row 0 the character frame's position, row 1 the rig root bone's position within that frame, row 2 the frame's rotation, row 3 the root bone's rotation within it, and rows 4 onward the remaining joints' parent-local rotations — a four-slot header, not the two-slot root-and-hips header some parameter docs describe.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/motion_field/field.py#L12-L14",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:271fe9c8b6df1ede2216f2622a0349137b30187adf46a2d82eba48cdd737c30e:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/motion_field/action_predictor.py#L116-L133",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0675af45a06cc8a167ef20904d0f73c3ea124c227161e63e45995605cc7e9671:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a116dc7f4b464bd092759d2ecf66ec93",
      "statement": "core/pose.py names slot 1 hips, but every consumer treats it as the rig's root bone rather than a Hips joint; the shipped database's bone 0 happens to be named Model:Hips, so the legacy name coincides with reality for this rig without being what the code means.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/pose.py#L10-L34",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:63242f2613e177445539dcbfec9cfc57589c9f55ecd3bb550068a45c7aba21cd:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/skeleton.py#L45-L66",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:cc33655c4dc2aa85ef9fdb9a06699a8a3a301f6435ac460b518a3b1b4bbaf460:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_660e5ba9d21d4aea859387c3fb90e0c0",
      "statement": "Angular rates are kept as rotation vectors rather than quaternions because a unit quaternion can only carry a rotation below two pi and scipy canonicalises it further, so round-tripping a radians-per-second rate through a quaternion silently aliases every joint turning faster than pi radians per second — about 2.6 percent of this dataset, of which the majority come back pointing the opposite way.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/pose.py#L192-L215",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2c0415723a4aa01ee3109a2a13ba78f4aba190c8fccec28cfdc4bcd3fa30171a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b42196d2437f401f817f1a0d73a5d9ca",
      "statement": "The character frame is reconstructed on the Python side rather than read from the file, from the same structural definition the C# side uses — reference bone zero and the bone-local axis that points forward in its rest rotation — which keeps one skeleton for the clips, the database and the rig and makes the frame exactly reproducible on both sides.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/simulation_frame.py#L1-L22",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ca13049528ef42e3ce075e496d07ff6ab65451f1da2dc621894c73653b334c13:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/formats/pose_set_importer.py#L35-L50",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a343f8560da3f99502bd44972f5b7c1e7d04acfeef3441674547287e48069375:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e8489ef7d10a423191314842eafb06fb",
      "statement": "The skeleton block is read from inside the pose file because the Python side has no ScriptableObject to read the bone tree from — names key the per-bone weight table, parent indices drive FK, and rest offsets give root-space positions — and keeping it in the same file is what stops the two drifting apart, which a separate skeleton file previously allowed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/formats/pose_set_importer.py#L35-L50",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a343f8560da3f99502bd44972f5b7c1e7d04acfeef3441674547287e48069375:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_940ccb6779e94b59af530202ef23869d",
      "statement": "Expressing a pose in the derived frame removes exactly the translation and yaw the motion field must not see, and applying it to an already frame-local sequence is the identity because the reference bone's own horizontal offset and yaw are zero by construction.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/simulation_frame.py#L164-L204",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:acd643710fc0f5e01a15b1ce744b2e53b7bebd41842012358bef6a567ceb5bf4:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_46889fcfb4934f0ea94763562af44975",
      "statement": "Every stored pose has a velocity including the last of a clip, because the extractor samples one frame beyond what it stores; stepping the last pose by its own stored rates inverts that exactly, which is what lets a derived rate exist for every stored frame rather than for all but the last, and keeps rates aligned with the poses rather than lagging them.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/simulation_frame.py#L286-L306",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0ee1dac64c2577405209edc1c1df74feca03431531d804256da5b6a0ab39d76e:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/simulation_frame.py#L307-L355",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a8d2dcefb36b32cde262b3d9394db4db195ac550224c993034baadc6ddf7541b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e487db9dadc7489c80ddc06e537f877d",
      "statement": "Frames are indexed globally across the database, so frame i plus one does not continue frame i across a clip boundary; the clip table is what maps global indices back to individual animations.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/pose_set.py#L6-L36",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:371d7c0635c347b3ac8652b56400373d14f8da9017a3bfbb4589eb6a758f5d2e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a057bfec74e7468091730a583827f8ca",
      "statement": "Neighbourhood blending uses normalised linear interpolation rather than spherical, because spherical interpolation handles only two rotations while blending a k-nearest-neighbour set means combining k at once; signs are aligned against the first quaternion first, since q and minus q are the same rotation and without that two near-equal neighbours can cancel instead of reinforce.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/quaternions.py#L1-L26",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0bee6f95d5a82cf6c58ab54c7fe6182de1a248efe35d38853c80b79e831ea050:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_08bf8387648f4e5ca7356ecd6c6683c0",
      "statement": "In forward kinematics joint 0 is the character frame, pinned to the origin with identity rotation, which is what makes the result independent of where the character stands and which way it faces; joint 1 is the rig's own root bone and carries the translation the pose stores.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://Python/core/skeleton.py#L20-L45",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2fb2a8e449c9a41434b3e0613bac9e409db7a2af02b0e3fd2f98c7999d850332:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
